ROAST.PET FRONTIER STACK SCANNER
2026-09-20 13:13 Asia/Phnom_Penh
1 useful find

ACCEPTED: StepAudio 3 Gen / StepAudio 3 family =E2=80=94 TEST

Sources:

   - StepFun launch coverage:
   https://tmtstatic.businessvalue.com.cn/news/8140509
   - StepAudio 3 Gen technical report: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12945
   - StepAudio 3 Music technical report: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16034
   - StepFun platform/docs entry referenced by current release coverage:
   https://platform.stepfun.ai/

Release/update date: technical reports September 11, 2026; StepFun publicly
launched the five-model StepAudio 3 family September 15, 2026; fresh
release coverage and documentation surfaced/updated through September 19=E2=
=80=9320.

What changed: StepFun now exposes a five-part production audio stack:
Realtime, ASR, TTS, Gen and Music. The Roast.pet-relevant component is
StepAudio 3 Gen: a unified general-audio generator covering zero-shot TTS,
voice design, vocal generation, sound effects, music, =E2=80=98vibe speech=
=E2=80=99, and
mixtures of these in one model. Unlike a conventional TTS-only endpoint, it
can create speech plus atmosphere/effects as a coherent audio scene. The
technical report describes a shared 16=C3=972048 RVQ representation at 12.5=
 Hz
and discrete autoregressive generation. Release coverage says all five
models are now on StepFun=E2=80=99s open platform.

Roast.pet capability: generate a finished comedy audio bed in one pass: pet
voice + laugh/breath/hesitation + sting + room/audience ambience + sound
effect, instead of separately calling TTS, SFX, music, mixing and timing
services. This is particularly relevant to the canonical =E2=80=98pet at a =
roast
podium=E2=80=99 format.

Inputs/outputs: natural-language/audio conditioning depending on endpoint
-> generated speech/general audio; Gen explicitly covers TTS, voice design,
vocals, effects, music and mixed audio. Music separately supports text/song
generation, dry-vocal accompaniment and iterative composition up to 5m30s
in the technical report.

API/install: hosted StepFun Open Platform. No StepAudio 3 weights were
verified as downloadable in this run; treat it as cloud-only for now.

Pricing: exact official Gen/TTS production tariff was not reliably verified
in this scan. Current third-party release coverage reports the Gen preview
as free and ASR around $0.24/hour, but do not budget from those figures
until confirmed against the live StepFun billing page.

Commercial license: hosted proprietary service; no open-weight commercial
license verified for StepAudio 3. Confirm StepFun API commercial terms
before production use.

Privacy/self-hosting: weaker than AuK/local alternatives. Customer-derived
scripts/audio leave our infrastructure; minimize payloads and avoid sending
original customer media when unnecessary. No self-host route verified.

Quality/latency constraints: vendor/research benchmarks are not Roast.pet
benchmarks. Unified generation may reduce editability: if the punchline
voice is excellent but the sting or ambience is wrong, regenerating the
whole scene could be worse than deterministic mixing. We specifically need
exact-script/name fidelity and controllable timing.

Exact Roast.pet use case: turn a structured RoastAudioSpec such as
{voice:=E2=80=98smug terrier=E2=80=99, line:=E2=80=98=E2=80=A6=E2=80=99, pa=
use_before_punchline:450ms, room:=E2=80=98small
comedy club=E2=80=99, audience_reaction:=E2=80=98short laugh=E2=80=99, stin=
g:=E2=80=98rimshot=E2=80=99} into a
complete 6=E2=80=9310 second audio scene before pet animation.

Smallest prototype: build 20 RoastAudioSpecs. For each, compare (A)
StepAudio 3 Gen one-shot mixed scene versus (B) current dry TTS +
deterministic local SFX/music mix. Score exact script/name accuracy,
punchline pause error in ms, voice quality, SFX appropriateness, mix
clarity, reroll rate, latency and total cost per accepted scene. Do not
test Music first; Gen is the directly relevant endpoint.

Decision: TEST. Do not adopt until one-shot acceptance rate beats modular
assembly.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW

   1. StepAudio 3 Gen vs modular TTS + SFX + mix for the podium-roast audio
   scene.
   2. AuK-Flash/local VoiceService for cheap controllable dry voice and
   delivery editing.
   3. PixVerse V6 / H3 / Wan / HD-V1 on the fixed hard-pet motion corpus.
   4. Qwen3.8-Omni semantic AV ProductionQC after deterministic gates.
   5. Low-resolution best-of-N video -> QC -> upscale only the accepted
   winner.

CHEAPEST MEANINGFUL CAPABILITY IMPROVEMENT
Local deterministic PetPrep/STT/QC remains the cheapest. For new creative
capability, StepAudio 3 Gen preview may currently be unusually cheap/free
to test, but that is not yet a production-cost assumption.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK THIS HOUR
A single audio model can now plausibly generate the pet performance,
paralinguistic delivery, comedy-club ambience, music and sound effects as
one timed scene. That attacks the audio-editing/orchestration layer rather
than merely improving voice quality.

CONCRETE STACK REPLACEMENT / SIMPLIFICATION
Introduce AudioSceneRenderer behind one interface:
RoastSpec -> AudioSceneRenderer -> {mixed_audio, optional dry_voice/stems,
transcript/timing metadata} -> STT/integrity gate -> VideoRenderer.
Backends: deterministic modular renderer (default), StepAudio 3 Gen
(experimental), future local unified-audio model. If StepAudio 3 Gen wins
acceptance-rate-per-dollar, it can collapse TTS + SFX selection + music bed
+ mixer for simple roasts; if not, the architecture stays unchanged.

No other newly released/materially improved item cleared the freshness +
Roast.pet relevance + verification threshold this hour; mature unchanged
tools were excluded.

